minal-daemon/src/crash.rs","sites":2,"reason":"the fault-injection recorder's fsync of its hit log: diagnostic state for crash-matrix evidence, not world state, so a crash mid-write loses a hit count and nothing recovery reconciles (M17.5 F-47 A05 brought fsync into the census)"},{"path":"crates/liminal-graph/src/store/log.rs","sites":7,"reason":"the checksummed append log's fsyncs (record, batch, directory): a crash leaves a torn tail that recovery truncates to the longest checksummed prefix (prop_torn_tail_truncates_cleanly), not state to reconcile (AM-17.8; F-47 A05)"},{"path":"crates/liminal-graph/src/store/snapshot.rs","sites":2,"reason":"the snapshot's fsync and atomic rename: a crash before the rename leaves the previous snapshot, after it the new one; either is a consistent state the log replays onto, nothing to reconcile (AM-17.8; F-47 A05)"}],"deferred_to":"M17.9","rationale":"ADR-0020 §5 requires exhaustive REGISTERED crash boundaries without stating the surface. AM-17.8 defines a registrable boundary as a point where a crash leaves state that recovery must RECONCILE. ILRP's eight protocol steps qualify. The runtime's ~30 other fsynced durable transitions are single checksummed log appends: a torn append is truncated to the longest checksummed prefix (liminal-graph store/log.rs, proven by prop_torn_tail_truncates_cleanly in crates/liminal-graph/tests/store.rs), so recovery is 'read the log' and there is nothing to reconcile. The deferred surface below is pinned by site count so this claim fails loudly if a new transition appears that is not a plain append."}
